WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:11.833
The next point is the placeholder, and it comes up a lot. A placeholder, as its name says, holds a place until the

2
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:21.167
main thing that will take that place arrives. So a placeholder in a text field is the text written inside the field that

3
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:30.500
tells me what I can write there, until I type the input I'll enter as a user; then the placeholder disappears as I type.

4
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:41.500
So for text fields, using placeholders isn't always the best choice. Why? Simply because as soon as we start typing they disappear, and I may want to see them again to remember the guideline

5
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:52.500
or the supporting text that was written as a placeholder and is now gone. To get it back I may need to delete everything I typed to read it again, especially if it held important

6
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:03.500
information. So it's always best to put the text we wanted as a placeholder above or below the text field like this, so it shows me what to write and doesn't disappear while I type.

7
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:12.500
In some cases there's also the floating label we talked about. That isn't a placeholder: it sits inside the text field, and when I start typing it moves

8
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:21.500
up, floating above the field or onto its border, and I can still see it while I type inside the field. So let's always try to avoid the placeholder.

9
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:29.833
One case that can confuse some people about placeholders is search. Search usually has its word written inside, and it isn't a regular text field: search isn't treated

10
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:38.167
as a text field. It's sometimes better to write the word Search inside it, so it's clear it's a search, and most of the time it sits at the

11
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:46.500
top of the website. If I tap it or start typing, the word Search goes away. So the same guideline that applies to text fields doesn't apply to it.

12
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:57.250
The next guideline we saw before when we talked about radio buttons: choosing the right field, or the right type, for each input I ask the user for. Like the case here that we discussed before: if

13
00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:08.000
I have three options, is it better to choose radio buttons or a dropdown? It's always better to choose radio buttons if there are few options and I have the space for it, because I'll see all the

14
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:18.750
options right away. If there are many options or I don't have enough space, I'll use the dropdown. So I think about which type of pattern fits as I ask the user for the input. I'll also

15
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:29.500
account for the size: if I ask the user for an email address, I won't give them a small text field; I need to give them a field big enough to type the email address, and so on.

16
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:39.575
The next point, and it's very, very important: showing the user which fields or inputs they must fill in to be able to continue, and which fields are optional, ones they don't have

17
00:02:39.575 --> 00:02:49.650
to fill in and can still continue. Here we can mark only the required items with an asterisk: I show the asterisk next to them so they understand they must fill it in.

18
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:59.725
Or I mark the optional item: I write in brackets next to its label that it's optional and they don't have to fill it. Or I do both, which may be better: an

19
00:02:59.725 --> 00:03:09.800
asterisk next to the required items and Optional next to the optional ones, so they can tell the two apart easily, without having to think a lot or try and get an error.
